  "explanation": {
    "id": "f9d6874a-5e7d-4c51-bdba-716a92d9c8cc",
    "body": "The pancreas secretes a bicarbonate rich fluid called pancreatic juice into the $\\textit{duodenum}$, not the stomach. That being said, the other options are all valid mechanisms to protect the stomach from the acidic environment.\n\n$\\textbf{Tight junctions between epithelial cells}$\nThis helps prevent acid from seeping in between the epithelial cells and damaging underlying tissues. Without them, acid would be able to get around the epithelial cells and wreak havoc.\n\n$\\textbf{Frequent replacement of damaged epithelial cells by stem cells}$\nThis helps ensure that the epithelial barrier stays healthy and protective. \n\n$\\textbf{Mucous coat}$\nEpithelial and glandular cells in the stomach wall secrete the mucous coat, which contains bicarbonate to help neutralize stomach acid. \n",
